1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:09,517
Japan, the early 1600s. A century of war has just ended, but the lords who fought it still rule their own domains.

2
00:00:11,017 --> 00:00:17,319
To keep them from rising again, the shogunate keeps their wives and children in Edo, as hostages.

3
00:00:18,819 --> 00:00:25,966
So on the roads it fears two things: a lord's family slipping out of Edo, and guns going in, to arm a revolt.

4
00:00:27,666 --> 00:00:32,058
It needs to see who leaves Edo, and to stop anyone who has no right to.

5
00:00:33,758 --> 00:00:40,394
Around 1619, it sets a checkpoint on the Tōkaidō, in the Hakone mountains, by Lake Ashi.

6
00:00:41,294 --> 00:00:44,878
Every traveller passes through its gate, between dawn and dusk.

7
00:00:46,778 --> 00:00:51,368
A woman leaving Edo shows a pass, written by officials in Edo Castle.

8
00:00:52,268 --> 00:00:58,725
It says who she is, where she's going and why, and describes her hair, and any marks on her face, hands and feet.

9
00:01:00,625 --> 00:01:07,457
A woman examiner checks her against it, and even unties her hair. If she doesn't match her pass, she goes no further.

10
00:01:09,357 --> 00:01:15,179
Nobody may go round: taking a side road past a checkpoint is a crime, punished severely.

11
00:01:16,679 --> 00:01:21,912
Most men need no pass at all. The checkpoint looks hardest at what the shogunate fears.

12
00:01:23,412 --> 00:01:29,265
So whoever leaves Edo by this road is seen and checked, and the road stays open to everyone else.

13
00:01:30,365 --> 00:01:35,090
The checkpoint stands for about 250 years, until 1869.

14
00:01:36,990 --> 00:01:41,056
At the utility, one account opens every door on the data platform.

15
00:01:41,956 --> 00:01:46,522
Its key sits in a script anyone could copy, and the log can't say who used it.

16
00:01:48,022 --> 00:01:55,110
So Ravi gives every job and every person a pass of their own, and every request is checked where the data is.

17
00:02:02,210 --> 00:02:08,606
In March, the regulator asks the utility a question: who read customers' bank details in February?

18
00:02:10,106 --> 00:02:14,589
Ama Owusu, the regulatory lead, asks the data team in a ticket.

19
00:02:15,889 --> 00:02:21,974
The platform's log has a line for every read. But every line names the same account: data_loader.

20
00:02:23,874 --> 00:02:31,650
It was made in the platform's first month. Now 47 jobs run as it, and four people sign in as it from their laptops.

21
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:39,814
Its key sits in a script in the repository, with no end date. Anyone who can read the script can use it.

22
00:02:41,514 --> 00:02:44,887
And it can read and change every table, in every domain.

23
00:02:46,387 --> 00:02:53,408
So nobody can tell the regulator who read the bank details. And anyone who copied the key could still read them today.

24
00:02:55,308 --> 00:03:00,935
Nobody meant harm. A new account took a ticket and two weeks, and data_loader worked at once.

25
00:03:02,435 --> 00:03:04,149
The safe way was the slow way.

26
00:03:07,849 --> 00:03:11,607
Ravi starts with identity: every request says who's asking.

27
00:03:12,907 --> 00:03:18,949
People sign in as themselves, through the utility's single sign-on, with a second check on their phone.

28
00:03:20,849 --> 00:03:29,785
Each of the 47 jobs gets an identity of its own. Each run is given a pass that lasts an hour, so there's no key to keep, or to copy.

29
00:03:31,885 --> 00:03:38,177
At Hakone, a pass described the one woman it was for. A key in a script works for whoever holds it.

30
00:03:42,077 --> 00:03:45,065
Then each identity gets only the rights it needs.

31
00:03:46,365 --> 00:03:52,413
The billing load writes billing's raw tables, and nothing else. The outage dashboard reads two tables.

32
00:03:54,513 --> 00:03:58,402
People get their rights through groups, by their role, never one by one.

33
00:04:00,302 --> 00:04:05,517
And customers' bank details can be read by two jobs and the billing team. Nobody else.

34
00:04:07,417 --> 00:04:10,327
A woman's pass named one journey, not every road.

35
00:04:14,227 --> 00:04:21,958
Every request is checked where the data is. Before a single row is read, the catalogue checks who's asking and what they may do.

36
00:04:23,858 --> 00:04:29,324
Being on the utility's network proves nothing: a laptop in the office is checked like any other.

37
00:04:31,024 --> 00:04:37,148
And there are no side roads: nobody can read the files under the tables without going through the catalogue.

38
00:04:39,048 --> 00:04:44,970
Data is encrypted where it's kept and as it moves, set once by the platform for every domain.

39
00:04:46,670 --> 00:04:49,655
As at Hakone, nobody goes round the checkpoint.

40
00:04:53,355 --> 00:04:59,699
Some keys can't go away: the password for an old billing system, or a supplier's key to its service.

41
00:05:01,399 --> 00:05:08,511
Those go in the platform's secret store. A job asks for one by name as it runs, so it's never written in the code.

42
00:05:10,611 --> 00:05:14,087
And every pull request is checked for keys before it's merged.

43
00:05:15,787 --> 00:05:21,233
The old key is cancelled, and six weeks after Ama's ticket, data_loader is switched off.

44
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:30,713
Now every line of the audit log names who asked: a person, or a job's own identity.

45
00:05:32,613 --> 00:05:35,554
In June, the regulator asks again, about May.

46
00:05:36,854 --> 00:05:42,872
This time the answer takes ten minutes: two billing jobs, and one billing analyst working on a ticket.

47
00:05:44,972 --> 00:05:47,170
Ama checks it, and closes her ticket.

48
00:05:51,070 --> 00:05:57,481
Why is this the platform's job? Left to each domain, the quick way would win again, as data_loader did.

49
00:05:58,981 --> 00:06:01,715
So the platform makes the safe way the easy way.

50
00:06:02,615 --> 00:06:09,359
A new job starts from a template, with its own identity, its rights, its secrets and its log from the first day.

51
00:06:11,659 --> 00:06:18,242
It takes minutes, not a ticket and two weeks. Since June, twelve new jobs, and not one borrowed account.

52
00:06:20,142 --> 00:06:24,633
How each lock is built, one by one, is the series Locks before the data.

53
00:06:28,333 --> 00:06:30,981
Here's the data practice, on Tomás's map.

54
00:06:32,281 --> 00:06:37,090
In the technology layer, the data platform's security is services of its own.

55
00:06:37,990 --> 00:06:44,565
There's sign-in through the identity provider, access checked by the catalogue, the secret store and the audit log.

56
00:06:46,665 --> 00:06:51,615
Every data product, and every job that builds one, uses the same services.

57
00:06:53,315 --> 00:06:58,720
And the rule is a principle: every request says who's asking, and is checked where the data is.

58
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:10,060
The checkpoint at Hakone didn't close the road. It looked hardest at the few the shogunate feared, and let everyone else go on.

59
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:17,648
On the data platform, every request is checked where the data is, by name, for only what it needs.

60
00:07:18,748 --> 00:07:21,757
Every domain gets that from the platform, by default.

61
00:07:23,457 --> 00:07:25,578
Next: one solution, end to end.
